    "question": "What is the difference between training data, validation data and test data?",
    "slug": "ai-engineer-what-is-the-difference-between-training-data-validation-data-and-test-data-9",
    "shortDescription": "Source Question AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Training data is used to fit model parameters. Validation data\nis used during development to choose models, hyperparameters,\nthresholds, or other decisions. Test data is held back until the end to\nestimate performance on unseen data.\n\nThe key principle is avoiding test-set leakage. If the test set\ninfluences model selection, it is no longer an unbiased final evaluation\nset.\n\nFor small datasets, cross-validation can provide more stable\nmodel-selection estimates, while a final untouched test set can still be\nretained when enough data is available.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Training data is used to fit model parameters. Validation data\nis used during development to choose models, hyperparameters,\nthresholds, or other decisions. Test data is held back until the end to\nestimate performance on unseen data.\n\nThe key principle is avoiding test-set leakage. If the test set\ninfluences model selection, it is no longer an unbiased final evaluation\nset.\n\nFor small datasets, cross-validation can provide more stable\nmodel-selection estimates, while a final untouched test set can still be\nretained when enough data is available.\n\nExample : Example: Suppose an online support application must answer\ncustomer questions. The engineer first defines the target metric and\nconstraints, builds a small representative evaluation set, implements a\nsimple baseline, measures its failure modes, and then adds complexity\nonly where the measured failures justify it.\n\nInterview Tip : Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Conceptual correctness, ability to explain\nfundamentals clearly, and understanding of when the concept matters in a\nreal ML/AI system.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Define the concept precisely, explain the\npractical reason it matters, and connect the answer to measurable system\nor model behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - Can you give a practical example? - What\nare the main limitations or failure modes? - When would you choose an\nalternative approach?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
