    "question": "What is an embedding?",
    "slug": "ai-engineer-what-is-an-embedding-12",
    "shortDescription": "Source Question AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "An embedding is a learned numerical representation of an\nobject—such as text, an image, a user, or a product—mapped into a vector\nspace where useful relationships can be measured geometrically.\n\nFor text, semantically related passages can receive vectors that are\nclose under an appropriate similarity measure. Embeddings are widely\nused for semantic search, recommendation, clustering, retrieval, and\nretrieval-augmented generation.\n\nAn embedding is not simply a list of human-readable features; it is a\nlearned representation whose dimensions generally do not have\nindividually interpretable meanings.",
    "detailedAnswer": "An embedding is a learned numerical representation of an\nobject—such as text, an image, a user, or a product—mapped into a vector\nspace where useful relationships can be measured geometrically.\n\nFor text, semantically related passages can receive vectors that are\nclose under an appropriate similarity measure. Embeddings are widely\nused for semantic search, recommendation, clustering, retrieval, and\nretrieval-augmented generation.\n\nAn embedding is not simply a list of human-readable features; it is a\nlearned representation whose dimensions generally do not have\nindividually interpretable meanings.\n\nExample : Example: Store an embedding for “wireless noise-cancelling\nheadphones” and compare it with the embedding for “Bluetooth headphones\nwith active noise reduction.” A semantic retrieval system can recognize\ntheir related meaning even though the wording differs.\n\nInterview Tip : Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Define the concept precisely, explain the\npractical reason it matters, and connect the answer to measurable system\nor model behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - Can you give a practical example? - What\nare the main limitations or failure modes? - When would you choose an\nalternative approach?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
