    "question": "What is the difference between prompt engineering and fine-tuning?",
    "slug": "ai-engineer-what-is-the-difference-between-prompt-engineering-and-fine-tuning-16",
    "shortDescription": "Source Question AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Prompt engineering changes the instructions, examples, context,\noutput format, or other input supplied to a model while leaving model\nweights unchanged. It is usually fast to iterate and works well when the\ndesired behavior can be expressed through instructions and retrieved\ncontext.\n\nFine-tuning updates model parameters using task-specific training data.\nIt can improve consistency, style, or specialized behavior when\nprompting alone is insufficient and sufficient high-quality examples\nexist.\n\nA practical decision starts with the cheapest reliable intervention:\nimprove prompting/context and evaluation first, then consider\nfine-tuning when a stable behavioral gap remains. RAG is often\npreferable when the missing information is changing external knowledge\nrather than behavior.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Prompt engineering changes the instructions, examples, context,\noutput format, or other input supplied to a model while leaving model\nweights unchanged. It is usually fast to iterate and works well when the\ndesired behavior can be expressed through instructions and retrieved\ncontext.\n\nFine-tuning updates model parameters using task-specific training data.\nIt can improve consistency, style, or specialized behavior when\nprompting alone is insufficient and sufficient high-quality examples\nexist.\n\nA practical decision starts with the cheapest reliable intervention:\nimprove prompting/context and evaluation first, then consider\nfine-tuning when a stable behavioral gap remains. RAG is often\npreferable when the missing information is changing external knowledge\nrather than behavior.\n\nExample : Example: A company evaluates a base model with a carefully\ndesigned prompt on 1,000 labeled support queries. It then evaluates a\nLoRA-adapted version on the same held-out set. If the adapted model\nimproves the target metric enough to justify training and hosting costs,\nthe team can adopt it.\n\nInterview Tip : Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Compare against a strong prompting baseline on\nan isolated evaluation set; account for quality, latency, cost, and\nmaintenance.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - Why not improve the prompt or use RAG\ninstead? - What dataset would you use and how would you prevent\nleakage? - What metrics would justify deployment?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
    "explanation": "Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.",
