    "question": "What are tokens and why do they matter for LLM applications?",
    "slug": "ai-engineer-what-are-tokens-and-why-do-they-matter-for-llm-applications-19",
    "shortDescription": "Source Question AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Tokens are the units into which an LLM tokenizer divides input\nand output text. A token may represent a whole word, part of a word,\npunctuation, or another text fragment depending on the tokenizer.\n\nTokens matter because they determine context-window usage, latency, and\noften API cost. Long prompts consume more input tokens, and long\ngenerated responses consume more output tokens.\n\nAI engineers therefore pay attention to prompt size, retrieved-context\nsize, output limits, tokenization behavior for the target languages, and\ntruncation policies.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Tokens are the units into which an LLM tokenizer divides input\nand output text. A token may represent a whole word, part of a word,\npunctuation, or another text fragment depending on the tokenizer.\n\nTokens matter because they determine context-window usage, latency, and\noften API cost. Long prompts consume more input tokens, and long\ngenerated responses consume more output tokens.\n\nAI engineers therefore pay attention to prompt size, retrieved-context\nsize, output limits, tokenization behavior for the target languages, and\ntruncation policies.\n\nExample : Example: A long RAG prompt contains 15,000 tokens of retrieved\ntext plus conversation history. Reducing retrieval to the most relevant\n4,000 tokens lowers cost and latency while preserving answer quality, as\nconfirmed by the evaluation set.\n\nInterview Tip : Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Define the concept precisely, explain the\npractical reason it matters, and connect the answer to measurable system\nor model behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - Can you give a practical example? - What\nare the main limitations or failure modes? - When would you choose an\nalternative approach?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
