    "question": "Explain how RAG works.",
    "slug": "ai-engineer-explain-how-rag-works-23",
    "shortDescription": "Reported AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) combines information\nretrieval with generation. Instead of asking an LLM to answer only from\nits parameters, the application first retrieves relevant external\ncontent and then supplies that evidence to the model as context.\n\nA typical RAG flow is: ingest documents → clean and chunk them →\ngenerate embeddings → index chunks → embed the user query → retrieve\ncandidates → optionally rerank/filter them → build a grounded prompt →\ngenerate an answer → evaluate the result.\n\nRAG can improve freshness and grounding without retraining the model for\nevery document update, but it can still fail when retrieval is poor,\ncontext is noisy, permissions are wrong, or the model ignores or\nmisinterprets evidence.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) combines information\nretrieval with generation. Instead of asking an LLM to answer only from\nits parameters, the application first retrieves relevant external\ncontent and then supplies that evidence to the model as context.\n\nA typical RAG flow is: ingest documents → clean and chunk them →\ngenerate embeddings → index chunks → embed the user query → retrieve\ncandidates → optionally rerank/filter them → build a grounded prompt →\ngenerate an answer → evaluate the result.\n\nRAG can improve freshness and grounding without retraining the model for\nevery document update, but it can still fail when retrieval is poor,\ncontext is noisy, permissions are wrong, or the model ignores or\nmisinterprets evidence.\n\nExample : Example: A support assistant receives “What is the refund\nwindow for an annual plan?” The system retrieves the current\nbilling-policy section, filters it to documents the customer can access,\nand supplies that evidence to the LLM. The answer cites the retrieved\npolicy instead of relying only on the model’s memorized knowledge.\n\nInterview Tip : Treat this as a higher-confidence reported interview\nquestion, but do not describe it as guaranteed or universal. Explain the\ndesign/answer from first principles and be ready for follow-up\ntrade-offs.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nKey Points to Remember : RAG quality depends on ingestion, chunking,\nembeddings, retrieval, filtering, reranking, context construction,\ngeneration, and evaluation.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - How would you measure retrieval quality\nseparately from generation quality? - What would you do if the correct\ndocument exists but is not retrieved? - How would you control latency,\ntoken usage, and access permissions?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.\n\nSource Evidence : Exponent identifies this as a real interview question\nfrom Sierra candidate interviews.",
    "explanation": "Treat this as a higher-confidence reported interview\nquestion, but do not describe it as guaranteed or universal. Explain the\ndesign/answer from first principles and be ready for follow-up\ntrade-offs.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nSource Evidence : Exponent identifies this as a real interview question\nfrom Sierra candidate interviews.",
    "keyPoints": "RAG quality depends on ingestion, chunking,\nembeddings, retrieval, filtering, reranking, context construction,\ngeneration, and evaluation.",
    "commonMistakes": "- Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
