    "slug": "ai-engineer-when-would-you-use-an-agent-instead-of-a-fixed-workflow-39",
    "shortDescription": "Source Question AI Engineer interview question.",
    "sampleAnswer": "Use a fixed workflow when the steps, decisions, and\nintegrations are predictable. It is easier to test, monitor, control,\nand estimate for cost and latency.\n\nUse an agent when the task genuinely requires dynamic planning, tool\nselection, iterative reasoning, or adapting the sequence of actions to\nthe situation. The extra flexibility introduces more failure modes and\noperational cost.\n\nA strong design starts with the simplest workflow that meets\nrequirements and introduces agentic behavior only where it creates\nmeasurable value.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Use a fixed workflow when the steps, decisions, and\nintegrations are predictable. It is easier to test, monitor, control,\nand estimate for cost and latency.\n\nUse an agent when the task genuinely requires dynamic planning, tool\nselection, iterative reasoning, or adapting the sequence of actions to\nthe situation. The extra flexibility introduces more failure modes and\noperational cost.\n\nA strong design starts with the simplest workflow that meets\nrequirements and introduces agentic behavior only where it creates\nmeasurable value.\n\nExample : Example: A travel-support agent has separate tools for looking\nup an order, checking a refund policy, and creating a support ticket.\nThe application validates every tool call and only permits the\nticket-creation tool after the user is authorized for that account.\n\nInterview Tip : Give a direct definition first, then explain the\nmechanism, trade-offs, and one practical example. Avoid listing\nbuzzwords without explaining why you would choose them.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Understanding of modern AI/LLM concepts\nand whether the candidate can connect model behavior to a practical\napplication architecture.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Use agents for genuine dynamic planning;\nconstrain tools, validate arguments, cap steps, and monitor side\neffects.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - When would you replace the agent with a\ndeterministic workflow? - How would you prevent unsafe or duplicate tool\nactions? - How would you evaluate task success?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.",
