    "sampleAnswer": "A GPU credit management system should track tenants or users,\ncredit balances, reservations, consumption, and refunds/adjustments with\nstrong consistency for balance-changing operations.\n\nA typical design uses an authoritative transactional store for balances\nand an append-only usage ledger for auditability. Allocation should be\natomic: reserve credits before a job starts, decrement or settle actual\nusage when the job finishes, and release unused reservations.\n\nProtect against double spending with transactions or conditional\nupdates, idempotency keys, concurrency control, and clear handling of\nfailed/cancelled jobs. Add quotas, expiration rules if required,\nmonitoring, and reconciliation against actual GPU usage.",
    "detailedAnswer": "A GPU credit management system should track tenants or users,\ncredit balances, reservations, consumption, and refunds/adjustments with\nstrong consistency for balance-changing operations.\n\nA typical design uses an authoritative transactional store for balances\nand an append-only usage ledger for auditability. Allocation should be\natomic: reserve credits before a job starts, decrement or settle actual\nusage when the job finishes, and release unused reservations.\n\nProtect against double spending with transactions or conditional\nupdates, idempotency keys, concurrency control, and clear handling of\nfailed/cancelled jobs. Add quotas, expiration rules if required,\nmonitoring, and reconciliation against actual GPU usage.\n\nExample : Example: Twenty requests arrive within 8 ms. A scheduler waits\nup to 20 ms for more compatible requests, forms a batch of 32 if enough\nrequests arrive, and dispatches it to the GPU. If only 20 are waiting\nwhen the deadline expires, it sends those 20 rather than making users\nwait indefinitely.\n\nInterview Tip : Treat this as a higher-confidence reported interview\nquestion, but do not describe it as guaranteed or universal. Explain the\ndesign/answer from first principles and be ready for follow-up\ntrade-offs.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Architecture reasoning, requirement\nclarification, trade-off analysis, scalability, reliability, security,\nobservability, and the ability to justify engineering decisions.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Define the concept precisely, explain the\npractical reason it matters, and connect the answer to measurable system\nor model behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - What are the main bottlenecks and failure\nmodes? - How would you scale the design and monitor it in production? -\nWhat trade-off would you make if latency or cost became the dominant\nconstraint?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.\n\nSource Evidence : Exponent reports this as an OpenAI interview question.",
