    "sampleAnswer": "Design the pipeline as asynchronous stages: ingestion → file\nvalidation → malware/security checks → type-specific parsing/OCR →\nnormalization → document structure extraction → chunking → metadata/ACL\nextraction → embedding → indexing → verification.\n\nUse object storage for raw files and a durable queue or workflow engine\nto coordinate processing. Make each stage retryable and idempotent so a\ntransient failure does not duplicate documents or corrupt indexes.\n\nHeterogeneous documents require different parsers for PDFs, scans,\noffice files, HTML, and structured data. Preserve provenance,\npage/section metadata, permissions, versioning, and processing status so\nretrieval can trace results back to authoritative source content.",
    "detailedAnswer": "Design the pipeline as asynchronous stages: ingestion → file\nvalidation → malware/security checks → type-specific parsing/OCR →\nnormalization → document structure extraction → chunking → metadata/ACL\nextraction → embedding → indexing → verification.\n\nUse object storage for raw files and a durable queue or workflow engine\nto coordinate processing. Make each stage retryable and idempotent so a\ntransient failure does not duplicate documents or corrupt indexes.\n\nHeterogeneous documents require different parsers for PDFs, scans,\noffice files, HTML, and structured data. Preserve provenance,\npage/section metadata, permissions, versioning, and processing status so\nretrieval can trace results back to authoritative source content.\n\nExample : Example: A PDF invoice is uploaded. An asynchronous worker\nextracts text and page metadata, identifies the invoice structure,\nstores the original file in object storage, creates searchable chunks\nwith ACL metadata, generates embeddings, and indexes them for later\nretrieval.\n\nInterview Tip : Treat this as a higher-confidence reported interview\nquestion, but do not describe it as guaranteed or universal. Explain the\ndesign/answer from first principles and be ready for follow-up\ntrade-offs.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing : Architecture reasoning, requirement\nclarification, trade-off analysis, scalability, reliability, security,\nobservability, and the ability to justify engineering decisions.\n\nKey Points to Remember : Define the concept precisely, explain the\npractical reason it matters, and connect the answer to measurable system\nor model behavior.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions : - What are the main bottlenecks and failure\nmodes? - How would you scale the design and monitor it in production? -\nWhat trade-off would you make if latency or cost became the dominant\nconstraint?\n\nCommon Mistakes : - Giving a memorized definition without explaining the\npractical implication. - Claiming a metric, architecture, or model\nchoice is universally best without stating the assumptions. - Ignoring\ndata leakage, security, evaluation, latency, or cost when discussing a\nproduction AI system.\n\nSource Evidence : Exponent reports this as a Databricks interview\nquestion.",
